Z||000000||Philphine||07-27-2000||09:36 AM||||<BR>i haven't looked at this yet cause it happened on the way home from work, about 2:00 am, but i thought i'd ask any way before i start trying to figure it out.<P>i 'm leaving the industrial park area where i work about to get on the express way when i notice my temp gauge has climed up to the next notch past the 220 mark. i noticed it as i was pulling away from a light and as soon as i took of it droped down the mark before the 220. <P>i kept thinking i've somehow lost coolant because it kept swinging back and forth. after a few miles the swinging decreased and i turned on my heater just to help out. the needle steadied out and settled in about like normal even when i turned the heater back off. stayed that way for the rest of the way, about 12 mls., and stayed steady for the mile or so bettween the x-way and home. <P>so my first guesses are low coolant and possibly a leak or maybe a thermostat going out. i'll see when i get up and out. have to at least check before i go to work. thought i could swing by the ramada for a quick look see but not today i guess.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-27-2000||11:37 AM||||Yes, I'd check the coolant level first. My 87 SE had a tiny leak and it started doing exactly as you described when the level dropped to a lower than normal level.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||07-27-2000||11:58 AM||||Sounds like there is air in the system. Try burping it all out(run a search in tech section). This will fix the swinging temp guage needle, but you need to find out where the coolant went in the first place. Check oil, and inspect all lines.||||reg||1
